{:edb/db-before-t 591 :edb/db-after-t 592 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533904 50 #inst "2026-10-05T10:32:55.380Z" 13194139533904 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264893 13194139533904 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264910 13194139533904 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264900 13194139533904 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264903 13194139533904 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264907 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264894 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1089 "q-104129" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1100 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1113 #uuid "2ecb21d1-c065-5b6a-a4bd-5b61570cf37e" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264893 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist." 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264895 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264896 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264897 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264898 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264899 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1041 "selection" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264898 13194139533904 true] [17592186264894 1110 #uuid "5c8f2adf-6c82-5526-81db-8a54996ad34a" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264895 1034 "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264895 1111 #uuid "ec6185fa-5c73-5586-a0a9-4e4ccb03476d" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264896 1034 "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264896 1111 #uuid "72f66ccf-ef41-54c4-ad39-c6cc4b52a20d" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264897 1034 "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)>0" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264897 1111 #uuid "49a8b7af-e52a-57ae-8700-b3706d8389db" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264898 1034 "$f(3)$  does not exist" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533904 true] [17592186264898 1111 #uuid "956020a8-0cca-5229-8c7d-f27a91b81b6f" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264899 1034 "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264899 1111 #uuid "37e8b8c6-05f9-5f9a-a137-d6f5fac9db4f" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264901 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1089 "q-342128" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1100 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f(-\\frac{3}{5})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1113 #uuid "7dfe1c04-ab2a-5512-a89e-4792c29fa3e9" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264900 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{3}{5})={lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264901 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264902 13194139533904 true] [17592186264901 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533904 true] [17592186264901 1041 "field-1" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264901 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264902 13194139533904 true] [17592186264901 1110 #uuid "76626e07-2f2f-5702-b3fa-1a865730c2ec" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264902 1034 "7" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264902 1111 #uuid "6736a8c4-83e7-5a08-8122-a5fa3569f09c" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264904 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1089 "q-342241" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1100 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1113 #uuid "04895225-82a3-50b3-9415-2a00cdf9e287" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264903 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264905 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264906 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1041 "field-1" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264906 13194139533904 true] [17592186264904 1110 #uuid "92744f46-5054-52de-9c8e-4597c808d422" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264905 1034 "12" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264905 1111 #uuid "48016568-2b77-5d3b-afa6-a9cf880bab44" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264906 1034 "-12" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264906 1111 #uuid "945df1d3-7212-563c-b240-9ce35c9979be" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264908 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1089 "q-342244" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1100 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1113 #uuid "e227b23a-bc8f-5586-a176-238c4b7efefa" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264907 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264909 13194139533904 true] [17592186264908 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533904 true] [17592186264908 1041 "field-1" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264908 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264909 13194139533904 true] [17592186264908 1110 #uuid "8d267e84-08f3-52b8-af3a-00d7b8f9ba73" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264909 1034 "0" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264909 1111 #uuid "93493554-3828-527c-bbd2-eb64b57ecf5d" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264911 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1089 "q-48102" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1100 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ -2x, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1113 #uuid "16483a30-6f65-5f41-998f-ed4512f89b1d" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264910 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264912 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264913 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264914 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264915 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264916 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1041 "selection" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264914 13194139533904 true] [17592186264911 1110 #uuid "033ea7c7-58e8-5df4-a0d8-18ff2aaba1b8" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264912 1034 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533904 true] [17592186264912 1111 #uuid "32cd595f-f3df-511f-8fc4-c884f5a4245b" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264913 1034 "$f(2)$ does not exist" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533904 true] [17592186264913 1111 #uuid "a0ea26bc-8a4f-54b8-a034-57df54c96c0c" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264914 1034 "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533904 true] [17592186264914 1111 #uuid "960de3b2-f53d-58c4-aca7-321be1ad9945" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264915 1034 "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" 13194139533904 true] [17592186264915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533904 true] [17592186264915 1111 #uuid "aadc80df-023b-57fc-9851-5387